@node CMacs Office
@chapter CMacs Office: .docx, .xlsx, .pptx and OpenDocument, natively

Before this subsystem, an Office document in cmacs was a picture.
@file{lisp/files.el} routes @file{.docx}, @file{.xlsx}, @file{.pptx} and
the OpenDocument extensions to @code{doc-view-mode}, which shells out to
LibreOffice, renders pages to PNG, and shows you the result.  You cannot
grep it, edit it, diff it, or hand it to an agent---and on a host with no
system @command{soffice} you get nothing at all.

@code{--with-cmacs-office} treats all six formats as what they actually
are: structured documents you can read, edit and save without losing
anything.

The six are two vocabularies over one idea.  OOXML (@file{.docx},
@file{.xlsx}, @file{.pptx}) and OpenDocument (@file{.odt}, @file{.ods},
@file{.odp}) are both a zip package of XML parts---OPC in one naming, an
ODF package in the other.  One container layer serves both.

@menu
* Office Status::               What is implemented today.
* Office Entry Points::         Opening, reading and editing a package.
* Office Identity::             Asking a document what it is.
* Office Guarantee::            What a save does and does not change.
* Office Refusals::             Input the container declines.
* Office Architecture::         How the subsystem is put together.
* Office Requirements::         Libraries and optional LibreOffice.
* Office Testing::              The round-trip gate.
@end menu

@node Office Status
@section Status

Every phase is implemented: reading, editing, authoring, page preview
and the agent tool surface.

@itemize
@item
@strong{Read} any of the six formats as a readable Org buffer.
@item
@strong{Edit} cells, paragraphs and slide text, saving back with the
round-trip guarantee described below.
@item
@strong{Author} a @file{.docx} or @file{.odt} from an Org file.
@item
@strong{Preview} laid-out pages, including on hosts where LibreOffice is
only available as a flatpak---which stock @code{doc-view} cannot find.
@item
@strong{Hand documents to agents}, which read and edit them through the
brigade tool bus.
@end itemize

Editing is surgical: @code{cmacs-office-set-cell},
@code{cmacs-office-set-block} and @code{cmacs-office-set-slide-text}
each rewrite exactly one element of one part, leaving every other part
byte-identical.  Authoring copies a template document and replaces its
body, so output inherits styles someone actually designed rather than a
generated stylesheet.

One limit worth stating up front: parts are parsed as trees, so a part
larger than 64@tie{}MB is refused rather than read or edited.  There is
no streaming reader.  The refusal names the part and its size---it is
never a silent truncation.

The detailed reference is @file{doc_org/cmacs/office/}.

@code{C-x C-f report.docx} opens the projection directly: the six
extensions are claimed from @code{doc-view-mode} by an
@code{auto-mode-alist} entry placed ahead of it.  Spreadsheets render as
one Org table per sheet, presentations as an outline with one heading
per slide.

The buffer is a @emph{projection}, not the document.  The document stays
open behind it as a handle, and edits go through that handle rather than
through the Org text---which is never re-parsed to reconstruct the
original.  Projecting a format we only partly understand is safe
precisely because the projection is never the thing we save.

In a spreadsheet projection, @kbd{e} edits the cell at point, @kbd{=}
evaluates its formula, and @kbd{C-x C-s} writes the document back.

Structured access is available too: @code{cmacs-office-blocks} for
paragraphs and slide shapes, @code{cmacs-office-cells} for addressed
cells, and @code{cmacs-office-text} for the whole document as one
searchable string.  Every block carries a writeback anchor---the part it
came from, a stable id the file provides, and its ordinal within that
part.

@node Office Entry Points
@section Entry points

Everything is handle-based, in the style of @code{cmacs-imgedit} and
@code{cmacs-vidstudio}: @code{cmacs-office-open} returns an integer
handle, and every other primitive takes it as its first argument.

@example
(let ((h (cmacs-office-open "~/Documents/report.docx")))
  (unwind-protect
      (progn
        (cmacs-office-part-names h)
        (cmacs-office-part-size  h "word/document.xml")
        (cmacs-office-part-bytes h "word/document.xml"))
    (cmacs-office-close h)))
@end example

Editing is deferred; nothing touches the file until you save.

@example
(let ((h (cmacs-office-open "report.docx")))
  (unwind-protect
      (let ((xml (cmacs-office-part-bytes h "word/document.xml")))
        (cmacs-office-set-part-bytes
         h "word/document.xml"
         (replace-regexp-in-string "Q3" "Q4" xml))
        (cmacs-office-save h))
    (cmacs-office-close h)))
@end example

Parts are raw bytes, so Emacs's own XML machinery does the parsing:

@example
(with-temp-buffer
  (set-buffer-multibyte nil)
  (insert (cmacs-office-part-bytes h "word/document.xml"))
  (libxml-parse-xml-region (point-min) (point-max)))
@end example

The complete primitive reference---every argument, every error
condition---is @file{doc_org/cmacs/office/reference.org}.

@node Office Identity
@section Asking a document what it is

@example
(let ((h (cmacs-office-open "report.docx")))
  (unwind-protect
      (list (cmacs-office-format h)      ; => docx
            (cmacs-office-kind h)        ; => text
            (cmacs-office-family h)      ; => ooxml
            (cmacs-office-main-part h)   ; => "word/document.xml"
            (cmacs-office-metadata h))
    (cmacs-office-close h)))
@end example

All of this is read from the @emph{container}, never from the file name,
so a @file{.docx} someone renamed @file{.xlsx} still reports @code{docx}.
Attachments and downloads get renamed constantly; trusting the extension
would be trusting the least reliable thing about the file.

@code{cmacs-office-kind} is the one to branch on.  A @file{.docx} and an
@file{.odt} are both @code{text}, and everything above the codec layer
treats them identically---which is what keeps six formats from becoming
six implementations.

The body part is @emph{resolved}, not assumed.  Nothing requires a
@file{.docx} to call its body @file{word/document.xml}, so it is looked
up through the package relationships, which you can walk yourself:

@example
(cmacs-office-relationships h)                       ; the package root
(cmacs-office-relationships h "word/document.xml")   ; what the body points at
@end example

Each relationship is a plist with @code{:id}, @code{:type},
@code{:target} and @code{:external}.  A non-external target comes back
already resolved against the directory of the part that declared it, so
the body's reference to @file{styles.xml} reads as
@file{word/styles.xml}.  OpenDocument has no relationship graph and
returns nil; use @code{cmacs-office-content-type} and its manifest
instead.

@code{cmacs-office-metadata} normalises both families onto one set of
keys---@code{:title}, @code{:creator}, @code{:created},
@code{:generator} and friends---so asking who wrote a document never
requires knowing its format.

@code{cmacs-office-known-formats} returns the codec registry itself: a
format appears there exactly when this build can identify it, which
makes it the honest answer to what is supported.

@node Office Guarantee
@section The guarantee

Two properties, both enforced by tests over all six formats, and
everything else rests on them.

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Saving a document you did not edit does not change a single
byte.}  Not ``reopens fine''---byte-identical.  @code{cmacs-office-save}
on a clean handle is a no-op by construction.

@item
@strong{Editing one part leaves every other part bit-for-bit
identical}---same bytes, same CRC, same compression method, with
inflation and recompression never entering the picture.
@end enumerate

The second is the one with teeth.  It means a feature this build does not
understand---SmartArt, a macro, an embedded OLE object, a custom XML
part, a chart, a signature---is never parsed and never regenerated, and
so cannot be damaged by an edit somewhere else in the document.

That is what makes it @emph{safe} to support these formats partially.
Full OOXML fidelity is a decade of work and is explicitly not a goal
here; this model is what lets coverage grow one feature at a time without
ever putting the rest of the document at risk.

@file{doc_org/cmacs/office/fidelity.org} is the honest account of what
round-trips and what does not, including the limits: rewriting a part is
unvalidated, relationships and content types are not maintained for you,
and spreadsheet sheet parts will be regenerated rather than surgically
edited when Phase 3 lands.

@node Office Refusals
@section What is deliberately refused

An OpenDocument package is only valid if it begins with an uncompressed
@file{mimetype} entry.  Untouched entries keep their position and
compression method, so that holds for free---right up until something
rewrites @file{mimetype}, which would move it to the end and deflate it,
silently producing a package strict readers reject.
@code{cmacs-office-set-part-bytes} and @code{cmacs-office-delete-part}
therefore refuse that part outright.

Part names are validated before any lookup: absolute paths, @samp{..}
components, backslashes and colons are rejected.  These files routinely
arrive as mail attachments, so the names inside them are untrusted input.
Inflated-size caps bound a decompression bomb, and a member that declares
one size and then delivers more is treated as a corrupt archive.

@node Office Architecture
@section Architecture

Three translation-unit classes, the same split @code{cmacs-roamgraph}
uses:

@multitable @columnfractions .30 .30 .40
@headitem File @tab Sees @tab Role
@item @file{cmacs-office-zip.c}
@tab glib, gio, libzip
@tab The package container.  No Emacs headers at all.
@item @file{cmacs-office-defuns.c}
@tab @file{lisp.h} and the bridge headers
@tab The only Lisp boundary.
@item @file{cmacs-office-init.c}
@tab @file{lisp.h}
@tab @code{syms_of_} / @code{init_} aggregation.
@end multitable

The container never sees @file{lisp.h}, so it is testable with no Lisp
VM; the Lisp boundary never sees @file{zip.h}, so the container stays
replaceable.  Documents cross into Lisp as integer handles, never as a
@code{Lisp_Object} stored in GLib-allocated memory---that would be
invisible to the garbage collector and crash it.

The design factors six formats into one container, three document models
and six thin codecs, rather than six independent implementations.  Adding
RTF or legacy @file{.doc} later is one new codec.  Details are in
@file{doc_org/cmacs/office/architecture.org}.

@node Office Requirements
@section Requirements

@code{--with-cmacs-office} needs @code{libzip} and @code{libxml2}
(pkg-config @code{libzip} and @code{libxml-2.0}), plus glib, gobject and
gio.  On Fedora that is @code{libzip-devel} and @code{libxml2-devel};
@file{install-deps} carries them in @code{PKGS_OFFICE} for all five
supported platforms.

libzip is not incidental.  It is chosen because it copies untouched
members through @emph{without re-deflating them}, which is what makes the
guarantee above both lossless and cheap---a one-word edit to a deck
carrying 100@tie{}MB of embedded video does not recompress the video.

LibreOffice is @emph{not} required and is never authoritative.  It is
used only for page preview and for legacy formats the native codecs do
not cover, and it is discovered via @env{PATH} or via flatpak
(@code{org.libreoffice.LibreOffice}, @code{com.collaboraoffice.Office}).

@node Office Testing
@section Testing

@file{test/cmacs/cmacs-office-tests.el}, driven by real
LibreOffice-produced fixtures in @file{test/cmacs/fixtures/office/}
rather than hand-rolled zips, so they carry the structure real files
have---including the ODF stored-and-first @file{mimetype} rule that a
naive rewrite silently breaks.

@example
make -C test check-cmacs
@end example

The round-trip gate runs over all six formats.  Both properties above are
asserted per format, along with the @file{mimetype} guard, the traversal
rejections, and stale handle behaviour.
